{
 "date": "2026-10-05",
 "diagnosis": "Four velocity-policy walks without a handoff and four with it: every policy tracks the requested 30 mm while commanded and stands after the emergency stop. isaaclab_velocity_rough is confirmed forward with and without the standing handoff and refuted in reverse both ways (-55.39 mm / -46.81 mm after completion); isaaclab_velocity_flat is refuted by post-completion yaw with and without it (0.123 rad peak either way). The per-step physics logs (post-completion-analysis.json) show each walk completing mid-stride at 0.09-0.23 m/s because the recipe commands 0.3 m/s for 30 mm; the network holding the zero twist afterwards needs 0.7-0.9 s and 15-26 mm to rest in reverse, 4-7 mm less with the handoff. Standing-only foundations do not drift (yaw within 0.02 rad over 4 s). The residue is braking at zero command, not standing; the handoff is an opt-in candidate, not a remedy. rough_walk_e braked in 0.28 s and 5 mm from the same speed in usd-reverse01, so braking is a policy behaviour. No threshold was changed.",
 "diagnosis_initial_2026_10_05": "Both velocity policies (rough_walk_e and the Isaac Lab velocity_flat export) track the requested 30 mm during execution and stand after the emergency stop, but keep stepping, drifting or advancing while the controller holds a zero twist after completion; the post-completion distance outcome refutes three of four walks. The Isaac Lab task trains with rel_standing_envs=0.02, so standing on a zero command is barely in distribution. The handoff after completion (zero-twist walking policy versus a hold or a standing policy) is the open design item; no threshold was changed.",
